## Hypothesis
Marketing materials claim the new Wilson Blade has "Direct Connect" technology for better feel. Structurally, this implies a variation in polymer modulus between the handle and the hoop.

## Methodology
Using a **Shore A Durometer** (for elastomers) and **Shore D Durometer** (for structural resins), I mapped the hardness at 3 key zones:
1. The PU Foam Handle
2. The Grommet Strip
3. The Carbon Matrix (Hoop)

## Data Analysis

| Zone | Shore A (Soft) | Shore D (Hard) | Material Classification |
|:---|:---:|:---:|:---|
| Handle Foam | 45A | - | Soft Elastomer |
| Grommets | 95A | 48D | Semi-Rigid Nylon |
| Hoop Resin | - | 85D | Cured Epoxy Matrix |

## Conclusion
The data reveals a stark "Hardness Gradient." The player touches a material (45A) that is **~40% softer** than the frame (85D). This confirms that "feel" is largely a function of the handle assembly, not just the frame flex.